# =============================================================================
# Pluck Live — environment variables
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to `.env.local` for local development and fill in the values.
# `.env.local` is git-ignored. Never commit real credentials.
#
# The application has two data sources:
#   * Supabase configured  -> the production backend (PostgreSQL + RLS)
#   * Supabase NOT configured -> the migrated legacy dataset (read-only),
#     clearly labelled on every page that shows prices.
# =============================================================================

# --- Site ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The canonical origin. Used for sitemap.xml, robots.txt, Open Graph URLs and
# the Supabase auth redirect allow-list. Baked into the build.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Supabase (browser-safe) ----------------------------------------------
# The publishable (formerly "anon") key. It ships in the browser bundle and is
# safe to expose only because Row Level Security decides what it can read.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# --- Supabase (server-only) -----------------------------------------------
# The service-role key bypasses RLS. It is used only on the server, by the
# API-key verifier, the usage logger and the agent provisioning action. It
# must NEVER reach the browser and must never be written to a log. If it
# leaks: rotate it immediately, then review audit_logs and api_usage_logs.
#
# The build fails loudly if a client component imports the module that reads
# this variable: `src/lib/supabase/admin.ts` is marked `import 'server-only'`
# and `tests/security/client-bundle.test.ts` asserts the guard.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Scheduled jobs (optional) ---------------------------------------------
# The shared secret the platform cron sends as `Authorization: Bearer <secret>`
# to POST /api/jobs/run. Without it the endpoint refuses every request, so an
# unconfigured deployment cannot have its jobs triggered by anyone.
#
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -hex 32
JOB_RUNNER_SECRET=

# --- Notification providers (optional) ------------------------------------
# The in-app inbox is always on. The external channels below are adapters: a
# notification is only ever marked as delivered on one of them when the
# provider itself confirms it. Leave them empty and the service reports each
# channel as "not configured" rather than pretending to deliver.
#
# NOTIFICATION_EMAIL_PROVIDER=resend
# NOTIFICATION_EMAIL_API_KEY=
# NOTIFICATION_SMS_PROVIDER=termii
# NOTIFICATION_SMS_API_KEY=
# NOTIFICATION_WHATSAPP_PROVIDER=meta
# NOTIFICATION_WHATSAPP_TOKEN=

# --- Local migration validator (optional) ---------------------------------
# Port used by the throwaway PostgreSQL instance that `scripts/validate_migrations.sh`
# starts. It is not the production database.
PGPORT=55432
